  "query_preview": "..\n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nRidging aloft will maintain the hazy, very warm, and dry \nconditions for at least the next few days. The main area of \ninstability and associated shower and thunderstorm activity will \ngenerally stay north of the Canadian border during this period. \nSeasonably breezy conditions will continue with surface boundaries\nbringing transient gusty northerly wind shifts, mostly over \ncentral/north-central MT. \n\nA trough begins to move southeastward through BC/AB and into the \nNorthern Rockies sometime Wednesday into Thursday. There are some \ndisagreements with the evolution of this system, but PWATs look to\nrise towards the three quarter to one inch range by late \nWednesday into Thursday according to the North American Ensemble \nForecast System (NAEFS). Shower and thunderstorm activity looks to\nincrease from north to south with areas along the Hi-Line seeing \nscattered showers and storms as early as Tuesday evening. The \nWednesday night through Thursday time period is generally favored \nto see the most widespread rainfall, especially for north-central \nareas. While some areas may see beneficial rains and cooling \ntemperatures, the convective nature of this activity may also \nbring some strong to severe storms with localized strong gusty \nwinds, hail, and heavy downpours. With that being said, ML CAPE \ndoesn't looks overly impressive at this point with values \ngenerally maxing out between 300 and 800 J/kg.\n\nThe ridging aloft begins to build back in heading into next \nweekend, but another trough approaching the Pacific NW coast will \nbring southwesterly flow aloft within striking distance of the \nNorthern Rockies. This will result in temperatures warming back \nup, along with daily shower and thunderstorm activity. - RCG\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nBreezy conditions, shifting winds, and localized elevated fire \nconcerns...\n\nThe combination of very warm, dry, and breezy conditions will \ncause localized fire weather conditions for the next couple of \ndays. While wind gusts will generally be below the 20 to 25 mph \nthreshold, surface boundaries will bring daily northerly wind \nshifts, most notably over central and north-central MT. Afternoon \nhumidity will fall to or near critically low levels with only \nmoderate to poor overnight recoveries, driest over Southwest \nMontana. There are no plans for fire weather highlights at this \ntime; however, those working to contain ongoing fires should \nbeware of dry conditions and breezy, shifting winds.\n\nIncreased shower and thunderstorm activity and cooler \ntemperatures Wednesday through Friday...\n\nThere has been ensemble disagreements and poor run to run \nconsistency for this time period. The primary question is how far \nsouth this trough will go, which will determine how widespread the\nshowers will be and whether temperatures cool below average. In \naddition to this uncertainty, there are also timing discrepancies \nwith a growing number of members shifting the most widespread \nprecipitation to Thursday into Friday rather than Wednesday into \nThursday. The cooler solution would support more beneficial \nwetting rains over central and north-central MT while the warmer \nsolution would support more scattered precipitation, but with \nincreased instances of stronger storms capable of localized strong\nwind gusts, hail, and heavy downpours. - RCG",
